WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.496
Installing PageMotor on SiteGround.

2
00:00:03.496 --> 00:00:13.287
SiteGround uses its own panel, Site Tools, rather than cPanel, and it asks one job of you that no other host does: taming its cache.

3
00:00:13.287 --> 00:00:16.922
Skip that one, and the install will feel haunted.

4
00:00:17.622 --> 00:00:26.120
The start is ordinary. Run the preflight check, then in Site Tools open File Manager, go into public_html,

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.808
upload the PageMotor zip and extract it there.

6
00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:35.741
You should end up with pagemotor.php, a lib folder, and config-sample.php.

7
00:00:35.741 --> 00:00:43.117
If you got a single folder called pagemotor instead, move everything inside it up one level.

8
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:49.223
Now the SiteGround part. Every site runs a full-page cache called Dynamic Cache.

9
00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:57.197
It is on by default, there is no off switch in Site Tools, and its only built-in manners are for WordPress and Drupal.

10
00:00:57.197 --> 00:01:03.549
Everything else, PageMotor included, gets cached like an ordinary page for up to twelve hours.

11
00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:07.873
That includes the install screen, the admin area, and the login.

12
00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:18.162
So put a kill switch in. In public_html, edit or create .htaccess, and set Cache-Control to private at the very top.

13
00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:24.113
Then flush the cache in Site Tools, under Speed, Caching, Dynamic Cache.

14
00:01:24.113 --> 00:01:31.304
The flush is not optional: SiteGround only applies .htaccess caching changes after one.

15
00:01:31.304 --> 00:01:37.173
To check it worked, load any page and look at the x-proxy-cache header.

16
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:43.124
BYPASS or MISS is what you want. HIT means the cache is still answering.

17
00:01:43.824 --> 00:01:49.207
The database works differently here too. SiteGround chooses the names for you:

18
00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:55.003
create the database and it generates one, create the user and that is generated too,

19
00:01:55.003 --> 00:01:59.489
with the password shown exactly once in a notice, so copy it now.

20
00:01:59.489 --> 00:02:05.699
And here is the step nearly everyone misses: a freshly created user has access to nothing.

21
00:02:05.699 --> 00:02:13.221
Go to Manage Access, pick your database, and give it all privileges, or PageMotor is turned away at the door.

22
00:02:13.921 --> 00:02:25.438
Then the same rename as any host. config-sample.php becomes exactly config.php, and that rename is what switches PageMotor on.

23
00:02:25.438 --> 00:02:34.029
Put the three generated values into DB_NAME, DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD. DB_HOST stays localhost.

24
00:02:34.729 --> 00:02:40.999
Finishing has a rhythm worth following exactly. Flush the cache, visit your site,

25
00:02:40.999 --> 00:02:44.559
then go to /admin/ and create your admin user.

26
00:02:44.559 --> 00:02:53.770
And straight after creating it: flush the cache again, clear your browser's cookies for the site, and only then log in.

27
00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:57.253
That one-two is what prevents the login loop.

28
00:02:57.953 --> 00:03:04.357
If you see “The user login cookie was not created”, your browser really did get the cookie.

29
00:03:04.357 --> 00:03:13.928
The problem is the next page, which the cache answers with a copy saved before you logged in, so PageMotor never runs and never sees it.

30
00:03:13.928 --> 00:03:22.162
Break the loop in order: log out or close the tab, flush Dynamic Cache, delete the site's cookies, then log in again.

31
00:03:22.162 --> 00:03:25.399
On SiteGround, when in doubt, it is the cache.

32
00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:38.280
One last thing, and it is SiteGround only. If you try to connect Claude to the site, SiteGround's Anti-Bot AI blocks the sign-in handshake, and you cannot fix it yourself.

33
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:46.686
It parks a challenge under /.well-known/, which is exactly where PageMotor publishes the documents Claude has to read.

34
00:03:46.686 --> 00:03:54.736
Three things make it hard to spot. It answers 202, a success code, so error-hunting checks call the site healthy.

35
00:03:54.736 --> 00:04:02.073
It is keyed to the requesting IP, so your own browser sees the correct JSON while Claude is challenged.

36
00:04:02.073 --> 00:04:09.268
And it leaves /mcp/ alone, which sends people hunting inside PageMotor for a fault that is not there.

37
00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:12.260
Only a SiteGround support ticket lifts it.

38
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:21.180
The guide has the exact .htaccess blocks, the optional rule that gives visitors the cache back, and the wording to send SiteGround's support.

